<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reza Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Reza Shah Pahlavi, Reza Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Reza Shah was an officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-cossack-brigade">Persian Cossack Brigade</a> who seized power in a coup in 1921 as Reza Khan. He became prime minister in 1923 and in 1925 the Majlis deposed the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> shah and made him shah, founding the Pahlavi dynasty. He built a modern army, railway and state, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> forced him to abdicate in 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reza-shah.jpg" alt="Reza Shah" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reza_shah_uniform.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 21 February 1921 <b>Reza Khan</b>, an officer of the Cossack Brigade, seized Tehran in a coup with the journalist Sayyed Zia al-Din Tabataba’i.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Amanullah of Afghanistan, in 1928 the first head of state to visit the Republic officially, drew on it; <b>Reza Shah</b> of Iran accelerated his reforms after visiting Turkey in 1934, imposing Western dress for men and restricting the veil; and in Tunisia Habib Bourguiba’s Personal Status Code of…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/kemalism">Kemalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>In September 1941 Allied forces occupied Iran after the Anglo-Soviet invasion that overthrew <b>Reza Shah</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ey-iran">Ey Iran</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Reza Shah Pahlavi</b> heard the song performed at a gathering in the home of his minister Teymourtash, he banned the second stanza in 1925, and it stayed banned until his forced abdication in 1941.</blockquote><a href="/songs/morq-e-sahar">Morq-e Sahar (Bird of the Dawn)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Reza Shah's</b> Pahlavi Iran disarmed the tribes, forced settlement and executed or imprisoned leading khans by 1934.</blockquote><a href="/state/bakhtiari-confederation">Bakhtiari confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the First World War, <b>Reza Khan's</b> government reasserted central control, and in 1922 Eqbal al-Saltaneh lost his position; he died in detention soon after.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-maku">Khanate of Maku</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Alasht, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.07,52.84&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Alasht on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5881 862 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Reza Shah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6331 1132h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6331 1132h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.07,52.84&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
